ARCHAEOLOGICAL, AND ALREADY HOMED TWICE. The removed narrative — ops as
collateral for working-tree-destroying commands, the approval messages a
`reset --hard` once ate, the per-path fixes that preceded the structural one —
is told better in `journal.py`'s module docstring, which also carries the
tracker IDs (WI-buhov, INV-dalup, INV-lovih) AGENTS.md omitted. The
`tracker-recover-disabled` marker mechanics and the `ORIG_HEAD` re-fire are
documented in `.githooks/reference-transaction`'s own comments, again in more
detail than here. So this fact had THREE homes and AGENTS.md was the one billed
to every agent on every turn — one fact, two homes, applied to the governance
file itself.

AND THE FRAMING DID GIVE IDEAS. The old text enumerated `reset --hard`,
`checkout` to a stale base and `clean -fd`, then said the problem is "now closed
**structurally**". In context that reads as reassurance about precisely the
commands this file prohibits elsewhere. Evidence it lands that way: earlier today
this agent ran `git checkout -- .agent/tracker-workspace/.ops/` — forbidden by
the Tracker section — reasoning that the ops were journalled. Where the
reassurance was, there is now the opposite: "the journal is not a licence to run
destructive commands on the working tree — the prohibitions above still bind."

KEPT, because each sentence changes what an agent DOES: where the journal lives
and that the hooks restore automatically; `tracker reconcile` as the command; and
the four cases the hooks do NOT cover — `clean`/`stash`, the naked merge/pull
abort on an untracked op, the SIGKILL marker leak (WI-pohir), and `rm -rf .git`.
Plus a pointer to `journal.py` for the mechanism and history.

Net: AGENTS.md 46222 -> 44050 chars, with nothing deleted that did not already
exist in a better place. mypy ratchet clean; smart-test skipped tests (docs only).

